<!--
    An ARM hard-float double parameter that the function only stores to a
    global (kuna_armfloatreturn.rs (in_memory)).

    armfloatargs_stored_{arm,thumb}.o (gcc -O2, recipe in armfloatargs_stored.c):
    put_g is `vstr d0,[r3]; bx lr` with r3 the address of g. The store to that
    resolved address becomes a copy of d0 into g, and nothing in the function
    reads g again, so the copy is the only read of d0. That copy is a read of
    the whole double: before, d0 was taken as two unread words and the C read
    an uninitialized d0. put_gv stores to a global declared volatile (no
    volatile range: the store is a copy too), put_two stores d0 and d1,
    and put_gf is the float control. put_ref stores d0 to a stack slot whose
    address it passes to use_ref; the call's effect on that slot carries the
    stored value, which is still a copy of d0. sel stores a double that a
    select merges (`vmovne.f64 d1,d0`) and zero_if one that an if replaces with
    a constant: a forward merge still stores the incoming double on the path
    that keeps it. call_put, which passes put_g the
    double it computes, needs put_g's stated prototype, so a callee-first
    decompile-all checks it (kuna-cli/tests/arm_float_arguments.rs).

    armfloatargs_merged_store.o (authored, recipe in its .s) is glibc's
    printf_size shape: scaled writes d5 only in a loop and stores it to a stack
    slot only on the paths where the loop ran. The incoming d5 reaches that
    store only through the loop's merge, on a path that never runs, so it is
    not a parameter, and neither are d0-d4 below it. put_mmio stores d0 to an
    address the script marks volatile, so the store is a write_volatile of the
    incoming d0, which keeps it too.
-->
